@h1 Interpolation & captures h1@

Wit has two substitution mechanisms, and keeping them straight is the single
most common point of confusion. @strong Captures strong@ —
@@@code ::name:: code@@@ and the body slot @@@code ... code@@@ — carry @strong
rich markup strong@, whole blocks of content. @strong Interpolation strong@ —
@@@code {{path}} code@@@ — drops a @strong scalar string strong@ into one narrow
context. This page shows what each becomes and, crucially, where each is live.

@h2 Captures carry rich content h2@

A captured value is not just a string — it is @strong re-parsed as a full
document strong@ at substitution time. So a captured value that is a bulleted
list becomes a real list:

@@@pre
#wrap ||body||
::body::
wrap#

@wrap
  body:
    @ul
    @li One li@
    @li Two li@
    ul@
wrap@
pre@@@

That renders a genuine @code <ul> code@ with two items (fixture
25-content-in-captures/value-contains-list). The same holds for a heading
(value-contains-heading becomes a real heading) and multiple paragraphs
(multi-paragraph-value). At an @strong inline strong@ position a
single-paragraph value splices its inline content, while a multi-block value
lifts into blocks (value-at-inline-position); an inline-only value takes a fast
path (value-with-emphasis-only).

@h2 Interpolation is a scalar hole h2@

@@@code {{path}} code@@@ is a doubled-brace @strong scalar strong@ hole. The path
is @code [A-Za-z0-9_.-]+ code@ (inner spaces allowed), and it resolves against
iteration variables, data definitions, or a bare scalar def. A number, boolean,
or null coerces to a string; a record or collection is
@code E_UNRESOLVED_REFERENCE code@ — you can only interpolate a scalar.

@h2 Where interpolation is live: only inside raw nodes h2@

This is the rule to memorise: @@@code {{…}} code@@@ interpolates @strong only
inside @@ raw-node bodies strong@. It is perfect for CSS holes:

@@@pre
#theme: { accent - green }

@@style
.badge { color: {{theme.accent}}; }
style@@
pre@@@

That renders @@@code .badge { color: green; } code@@@ (verified). In ordinary prose,
@@@code { code@@@ is record-arg syntax, so a brace pair renders @strong literally
strong@:

@@@pre
#book: { title - The Keeper }

The book {{book.title}} versus @book.title here.
pre@@@

That outputs the literal text @@@code {{book.title}} code@@@ next to the
resolved @code The Keeper code@ (verified). A frozen @@@code @@@ code@@@ body
makes @@@code {{…}} code@@@ inert too — see
@a |href /docs/guides/literal-raw-css/| Literal & raw nodes a@.

@h2 Two outputs, one lookup h2@

The same lookup feeds both mechanisms, but they emit different things:

@ul
@li @@@code {{name}} code@@@ → a scalar string, for class names, colours, CSS
holes — live only in @@ bodies. li@
@li @@@code ::name:: code@@@ and @@@code ... code@@@ → rich content, live only in
a definition body. li@
@li @strong Data into prose strong@ is @@@code @name.field code@@@ — never
@@@code {{…}} code@@@. li@
ul@

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li Expecting @@@code {{book.title}} code@@@ to interpolate in a paragraph — it
renders literally; use @@@code @book.title code@@@. li@
@li Writing @@@code ::loopvar.field:: code@@@ in prose — that is drift from older
notes; use data access @@@code @loopvar.field code@@@. li@
ul@

